// System.out and System.err are two different streams, as the standard-library
// table in docs/language.md says. System.err writes to descriptor 2.
//
// The harness merges the two descriptors into one pipe, so this program cannot
// see which one a line came from -- what it does pin is the *order*: a write to
// stderr has to reach the pipe after everything stdout had already buffered
// (see ty_ps_out in internal/runtime/src/tyrt2.c, which flushes stdout before
// switching descriptors). Without that flush the err lines come out ahead of
// the out lines here. The separation itself is checked by hand with
// `./program 2>/dev/null` and `./program 1>/dev/null`.
class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("out1")
    System.err.println("err1")
    System.out.print("out2-no-newline ")
    System.err.print("err2-no-newline\n")
    System.out.println("out3")
  }
}
